<div class="oc-video-container">
<p><a href="https://www.openculture.com/?post_type=post&amp;p=1128036"><img decoding="async" src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/bqOgAHtSANs/maxresdefault.jpg" width="800" border="0"></a></p>
</div>
<p>	<!-- /oc-video-embed -->
</p></div>
<p><!-- /oc-video-wrapper --></p>
<p>O’Brien has never made a secret of his Beatles obsession, but here he demonstrates it definitively by supplying most of the details of the band’s story himself. (That goes even for relatively obscure ones: he mentions, for example,&nbsp;the score Paul McCartney composed for the film&nbsp;<em>The Family Way</em>, <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/03/hear-paul-mccartneys-forgotten-1967-movie-soundtrack-arranged-by-george-martin.html">featured earlier this year on Open Culture</a>.) He also manages to win the trivia contest against<em>&nbsp;</em>Holland at Abbey Road Studios in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bqOgAHtSANs">the video just above</a>, but then, he’d been studying in one sense or another for quite some time. A decade ago, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Fs6Vynwcxfc">he held his own in conversation with Mark Lewisohn</a>, the <a href="https://amzn.to/4wMhbYs">first volume of whose painstaking three-part Beatles biography</a> comes to nearly a thousand pages in the abridged edition. That was on his YouTube interview series <a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLVL8S3lUHf0TIbLusugli-_6OhbvqgMvD"><em>Serious Jibber-Jabber</em></a>,&nbsp;the predecessor to his current podcast&nbsp;<a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLVL8S3lUHf0T3IR5qgs_IJ17ivx9T4M3k"><em>Conan O’Brien Needs a Friend</em></a>.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>Holland and his co-host Dominic Sandbrook<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5bPL9gR6mJs"> appeared on <em>Conan O’Brien Needs a Friend</em> last fall</a>.&nbsp;They may have seemed an odd choice in a guest roster consisting mostly of comedians, actors, and musicians,&nbsp;but O’Brien himself has declared <em>The Rest Is History </em>“the best podcast in the world,” and their shows’ fan bases turn out not to be mutually exclusive. That wide-ranging conversation went stimulatingly enough to inspire a completion of the crossover just a few months thereafter, when Holland brought O’Brien to Abbey Road for an hour and a half of nothing but talk about the historical significance of the Beatles, whom O’Brien declares “a complete break from what happened before.” (Sandbrook, the rare non-appreciator of the Fab Four, absented himself from the discussion.)</p>
