</p></div>
<p><!-- /oc-video-wrapper --></p>
<p>O’Brien&nbsp;praises books like Lewisohn’s, as well as Ian Leslie’s more recent <a href="https://amzn.to/3UFXns4"><em>John &amp; Paul: A Love Story in Songs</em></a>,&nbsp;but his understanding of the group as a historical phenomenon suggests that he’s also read the work of&nbsp;critic Ian MacDonald. After&nbsp;<em>Revolver</em>,&nbsp;“it seemed to Western youth that The Beatles <em>knew</em> — that they had the key to current events and were somehow orchestrating them through their records.” So MacDonald writes in his book<a href="https://amzn.to/45zTjfm"><em> Revolution in the Head: The Beatles’ Records and the Sixties</em></a>, whose title refers to “the true revolution of the Sixties”: an unconscious mental adaptation to the passage from a local reality of “consensus, hierarchy, and fixed values” to a global one of individualism, sensationalism, and “jealously leveled standards.” The Beatles didn’t cause those changes, as they themselves well understood. But they accepted the roles of its avatars, and more than 60 years after the rise of Beatlemania, we still live in the world they represented.</p>
